{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target states that in arid cities 'all incoming solar radiation is converted into sensible heat' because evapotranspiration is suppressed. This is physically false: even with zero latent heat, significant fractions of incoming solar are lost to reflected shortwave (albedo) and outgoing longwave radiation during the day, not stored. Overstating the partition to 100% sensible heat/storage mischaracterizes the energy balance and the magnitude of the nocturnal release.","premise":"The problem asks what single mechanism best explains the night-time UHI gap and its cross-city variation; the target's quantitative partitioning of daytime energy is material to that mechanism.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"5f4d3c2b1a0987654321abcedf","quote":"this latent heat pathway is suppressed. Consequently, all incoming solar radiation is converted into sensible heat, which is stored in urban surfaces and released after sunset."}],"target":"5ea5547acb9448144d807b741974b0ca7c635fd909f352ea21c1153558d9259c"}]}